//
// A REFERENCE NEEDS A PLACE, NOT A CONSTRUCTED TEMPORARY (`@FR-B-Ref-Intro`).  B-Ref-Intro: a
// `&` binding links the bound name to an LVALUE — a variable, a field, an element.  A struct
// literal is a value under construction, not a place, so `Add { l: &Lit { v: 1 }, … }` is
// refused, naming what `&` takes; the recursive shape is written through locals
// (`heap/H-RefHome/a-generic-recursive-enum-links-its-nodes-through-locals`) or with the
// children in a `vector<Expr>` (`heap/H-NewRec/…`).  The OCaml bar's A5b note; loft-lang/plans#202
// closed its P6 (binding a hidden local for the temporary) as not needed.  The second error is
// the first's cascade: refused as a link, the temporary is read as the record itself, which
// the `reference<Expr>` field does not take.
//
// @EXPECT_ERROR: requires an addressable operand
// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot assign Lit to field Add.l
//
// @falsified-at: none — a conformance guard; the refusal fires on 9121f1b50, interpret and native.
// CHANNEL: the compile diagnostic.
// ARMED: no instrument.
// WITNESS: the diagnostic names what `&` takes.
// HOLDS: the B-Ref-Intro cells beside it, the admitted places.

enum Expr {
  Lit { v: integer },
  Add { l: reference<Expr>, r: reference<Expr> },
}

fn eval(e: Expr) -> integer {
  match e {
    Lit { v } => v,
    Add { l, r } => eval(l) + eval(r),
  }
}

fn main() {
  two: Expr = Lit { v: 2 };
  e = Add { l: &Lit { v: 1 }, r: &two };
  assert(eval(e) == 3, "unreachable: {eval(e)}");
}
